From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: anxelok <anxelok@localhost>
Date: Fri, 19 Jun 2026 19:07:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] De-latch REFUSED and route off-owner events to the owner

Fix 0 (dominant root cause): apartment contention is a transient, per-dispatch
condition, but the contention branch in callThroughApartment called
recordRefusal -> noteRefusal -> escalate(Mode.REFUSED), permanently latching the
plugin's sticky volatile compatibility mode. Once latched, planFor returned REFUSE
for EVERY subsequent dispatch on EVERY thread (including the owner thread, where the
listener could run safely), silently dropping all of that plugin's events -- this is
how WorldGuard's protection listeners were bypassed (3366 events). The contention
branch no longer escalates the persistent mode (no noteApartmentContention /
recordStrictFallback / recordRefusal); when a dispatch genuinely cannot run it is
dropped LOCALLY via a new recordTransientRefusal that bumps the diagnostics counter
and logs type=transient_refusal but never mutates profile.mode, so the plugin is
fully re-evaluated on the next dispatch.

Fix 1c: callEvent now routes non-async events fired off-owner outside a staging
window to the owner via RegionTickStaging.deferEventToOwner before they reach the
apartment path, so post-facto events (ChunkLoad/Unload, notifications) dispatch on
the owner thread instead of being refused.

Serial / single-thread ticking is byte-identical: on the owner thread the apartment
is never contended, so the changed BUSY branch is never entered.


diff --git a/src/main/java/io/papermc/paper/plugin/manager/PaperEventManager.java b/src/main/java/io/papermc/paper/plugin/manager/PaperEventManager.java
index f19e7eab56f264b1cb7387c57da976bbe572b452..23fea5deafb8d19a27f989a457b477dd50baba9d 100644
--- a/src/main/java/io/papermc/paper/plugin/manager/PaperEventManager.java
+++ b/src/main/java/io/papermc/paper/plugin/manager/PaperEventManager.java
@@ -55,6 +55,16 @@ class PaperEventManager {
         if (!event.isAsynchronous() && net.minecraft.server.threading.RegionTickStaging.stageEvent(event)) {
             return;
         }
+        // Loom Fix 1c - the stageEvent path above only catches events fired INSIDE a random-tick / entity-tick
+        // staging window. Events fired on a region worker OUTSIDE that window (e.g. ChunkLoadEvent /
+        // ChunkUnloadEvent from the chunk pipeline, and other post-facto notifications) are routed back to the
+        // owning thread here, where their listeners run single-threaded. This replaces the old behaviour of
+        // running them inline on a worker and then REFUSING them on apartment contention (which silently
+        // dropped e.g. WorldGuard's protection listeners). Staged-veto events (cancellation gating a
+        // synchronous side effect) are intentionally NOT deferred and fall through unchanged.
+        if (!event.isAsynchronous() && net.minecraft.server.threading.RegionTickStaging.deferEventToOwner(event)) {
+            return;
+        }
         // Loom end
         if (event.isAsynchronous() && this.server.isPrimaryThread()) {
             throw new IllegalStateException(event.getEventName() + " may only be triggered asynchronously.");
@@ -177,15 +187,27 @@ class PaperEventManager {
         final CompatibilityApartment apartment = this.compatibilityKernel.apartmentFor(registration.getPlugin());
         try (CompatibilityApartment.Scope scope = apartment.enter(transaction, strictFallback, path)) {
             if (!scope.acquired()) {
-                profile.noteApartmentContention("contention in " + event.getEventName() + " lane=" + registration.getPriority());
-                this.compatibilityKernel.diagnostics().recordStrictFallback(
-                    profile,
-                    "apartment busy in " + event.getEventName() + " owner=" + scope.busyLease()
-                );
+                // Loom Fix 0 - apartment contention is a TRANSIENT, per-dispatch condition (another thread
+                // currently holds this plugin's single apartment lease). It must NOT permanently latch the
+                // plugin's persistent compatibility mode to LEGACY_STRICT / REFUSED: doing so dropped ALL of
+                // that plugin's subsequent events on EVERY thread (incl. the owner thread, where the listener
+                // could run safely), which silently bypassed e.g. WorldGuard's protection listeners. So we no
+                // longer call noteApartmentContention / recordStrictFallback / recordRefusal here (each of which
+                // escalated the sticky volatile mode). Instead the refusal — when we cannot run the listener
+                // for this one dispatch — is a LOCAL, non-persistent decision recorded for diagnostics only.
                 if (OwnerDomainExecutor.canRunSynchronousCallbacks()) {
-                    this.compatibilityKernel.diagnostics().recordRefusal(
+                    // The owner thread is hard-blocked in futures.join() during the parallel phase, so we can
+                    // neither run the listener inline here (off-owner, would race shared plugin state) nor
+                    // synchronously round-trip to the owner (deadlock). Post-facto events were already routed
+                    // to the owner by RegionTickStaging.deferEventToOwner before reaching this path; anything
+                    // still arriving here is the staged-veto class (cancellation must suppress a synchronous
+                    // side effect) which cannot be deferred without firing-site staging. Drop ONLY this one
+                    // dispatch (no persistent mode change), recording it for the diagnostics counter so the
+                    // remaining gap stays visible.
+                    this.compatibilityKernel.diagnostics().recordTransientRefusal(
                         profile,
-                        "owner-domain contention refusal for " + event.getEventName() + " lane=" + registration.getPriority()
+                        "transient apartment contention for " + event.getEventName() + " lane=" + registration.getPriority()
+                            + " owner=" + scope.busyLease()
                     );
                     return;
                 }
diff --git a/src/main/java/io/papermc/paper/plugin/manager/compat/CompatibilityDiagnostics.java b/src/main/java/io/papermc/paper/plugin/manager/compat/CompatibilityDiagnostics.java
index a9c393b7e563e78be3c17209453fe8064ba97628..95263f66f2515d9e07bcb8ccc7c35d0a1cb7698c 100644
--- a/src/main/java/io/papermc/paper/plugin/manager/compat/CompatibilityDiagnostics.java
+++ b/src/main/java/io/papermc/paper/plugin/manager/compat/CompatibilityDiagnostics.java
@@ -261,6 +261,24 @@ public final class CompatibilityDiagnostics {
         );
     }
 
+    // Loom Fix 0 - a PER-DISPATCH refusal that does NOT latch the plugin's persistent compatibility mode.
+    // Used for transient apartment contention: we drop only the current dispatch but leave the plugin fully
+    // re-evaluated on the next one (so an event firing on the owner thread, or after the lease frees, runs
+    // normally). It bumps the diagnostics counter and emits a distinct "transient_refusal" log so the
+    // remaining staged-veto gap stays observable, but it never calls profile.noteRefusal (which would set the
+    // sticky volatile mode to REFUSED and poison every future dispatch on every thread).
+    public void recordTransientRefusal(final PluginCompatibilityProfile profile, final String reason) {
+        this.refusals.increment();
+        this.logStructured(
+            "transient_refusal",
+            Map.of(
+                "plugin", profile.pluginName(),
+                "reason", reason,
+                "mode", profile.mode().name()
+            )
+        );
+    }
+
     public void recordAsyncViolation(final String reason) {
         this.asyncViolations.increment();
         final org.bukkit.plugin.Plugin current = CompatibilityRuntimeContext.currentPlugin();
